Model: Rewritten Excerpt:  
Most of the nitrogen on Earth is in the form of nitrogen gas, which plants and animals cannot use directly. In the early 1900s, scientists learned how to turn this gas into useful compounds that can help crops grow. This process is known as the Haber‑Bosch method, and almost all nitrogen fertilizers are made this way.

The Haber‑Bosch process takes nitrogen gas and mixes it with hydrogen gas (H₂). The mixture is then put under very high pressure—about 200 times the pressure at sea level. This pressure is similar to what you would feel if you were 2,000 meters (about 6,500 feet) deep in the ocean, or if you stacked more than six Eiffel Towers on top of each other. After the pressure, the gas mixture is heated to a very high temperature, around 450 °C (842 °F). Keeping the gas under such high pressure and heat requires a lot of energy. In fact, the Haber‑Bosch process uses about 1–2 % of the world's total energy each year.

