At night, the boat went out. It was dark, so it sailed without lights. Andrey stayed on the bridge the whole night. The water was calm. The stars were hidden by a light mist. Far to the south, an enemy searchlight glowed in the clouds.

The boat was going near a mine field. In the morning, the light turned greenish‑orange. The boat, called Kate, sank to a deep spot in the water. With only a compass and a map, the crew tried to move under the mines. Yakovlev steered the boat. Prince Bylopolsky checked how much the boat drifted and told the chief engineer. Andrey read the map and told the man at the wheel what to do.

The boat did not feel much movement. It seemed to stay still in the dark. Most of the men were lying on their backs, trying to use as little air as possible.

