 An ordinary weathercock with marked reference points can usually tell the wind direction during the day. However, to send its signals automatically to inside a building, we need other devices. Many of these devices use gears or friction that reduces how sensitive the instrument is, especially when the rod must reach several floors. Mr. Emile Richard, a waterworks inspector at Versailles, has designed a clever system that keeps the weathercock light but still very sensitive. His system has two main parts: a fixed part and a movable part. The fixed part is marked A and B in the diagram and forms the rod or support. An iron tube, called T, with clamps, called P, at its bottom, makes the base of the device, and after the device is set up it is hidden by a decorative zinc cover, called Z.

