 Human sleep has different stages that can be seen by looking at brain waves recorded with electrodes on the scalp, a method called electroencephalography. Dreams happen in all stages, but the most vivid dreams happen in a stage called REM sleep, where rapid eye movements occur. In REM sleep, all body muscles except the eye muscles are stopped by the brain, so we don’t act out our dreams. This is why moving around in a dream, like sleepwalking, normally only happens in non‑REM stages. In those stages, part of the brain is awake while the rest is asleep. Sleep talking follows the same pattern: talking needs fast muscle movements, so it usually happens in non‑REM stages when muscles are not paralyzed.

