The LCD uses a method called electro-optical modulation. This means it uses electricity to control how much light passes through it. Each pixel is a thin layer of molecules between two electrodes and two polarizing filters. The electrodes give power to the liquid crystal layer, but they do not block light. Light moves with a certain direction, called polarity. A polarizing filter only lets light with a specific polarity pass, similar to trying to fit a ruler through a narrow opening. The filter only allows light that matches the orientation of its opening. The two filters are positioned at right angles to each other, so the openings are in different directions. Without the liquid crystals between them, the filters would block all light, because any light that gets through the first filter would not match the second filter’s opening.

