 The phonograph was created after Thomas Edison worked on the telegraph and the telephone. In 1877, Edison was trying to make a machine that could turn telegraph messages into marks on paper tape. He could then send the tape again over the telegraph. He thought the same idea might work for telephone messages. He tested a diaphragm that pressed a small point onto fast‑moving paper made of paraffin. The sound from a person speaking made little dents in the paper. Later Edison replaced the paper with a metal cylinder wrapped in tin foil. The machine had two parts: one to record and one to play back. When a person spoke into a mouthpiece, the sound waves pushed the recording needle into the cylinder and made a groove that looked like hills and valleys. Edison showed a sketch of the machine to his mechanic, John Kruesi, who built it in about 30 hours. Edison tested the machine by speaking the nursery rhyme “Mary had a little lamb.” The phonograph then played his words back to him.

