 Japanese records say the first pottery was made in 660 B.C. But it wasn't until the Christian era that pottery improved a lot. In 1223 A.D., people made better pottery and decorations. From then until the 16th century, major pottery centers were founded in Owari, Hizen, Mino, Kyoto, Kaga, and Satsuma. The crackled ware, called Rahn-Yaki, first appeared in Kyoto at the start of the 16th century. The best old Hizen pottery is from Arita Hizen between 1580 and 1585. Old Satsuma pottery dates back to 1592. Consul-General Van Buren said that porcelain clay is found in almost all parts of Japan. The different types of clay are usually close to each other, and near canals and rivers, which is very useful because it allows transportation.

