Tool Call: compute_grade_level with args {'text': 'Rivers and streams carry water and also carry sediment. As water moves over the riverbed, it can pick up and carry sediment downstream. The sediment can be stuck to the bed, suspended in the water, or dissolved in the water. How much sediment moves depends on how much sediment is available and how fast the river flows. Rivers can also erode rock, creating new sediment from their beds and from the nearby hills. Because of this, rivers are thought to set the base level for landscape change in places without glaciers. Rivers connect different parts of the landscape.\n\nWhen rivers travel across the land, they usually grow larger by joining with other rivers. The collection of rivers becomes a drainage system. Drainage systems usually follow four main patterns: dendritic, radial, rectangular, and trellis. Dendritic is the most common, occurring when the ground is stable and not faulted. A drainage system has four main parts: the drainage basin, the alluvial valley, the delta plain, and the basin that receives the water. Examples of river landforms include alluvial fans, oxbow lakes, and river terraces.'}
