Model: Rewritten Excerpt:
Dr. Eder has spent a long time studying soda and potash developers, which can be better than the ammoniacal pyrogallol developer. This advantage is most noticeable when using emulsions that contain ammonia. These emulsions often produce a green or red fog with an ammoniacal developer, a clay‑colored fog when viewed from the side, and a pale purple color when you look through them. Ferrous oxalate works well on plates treated with these developers, as do soda and potassa developers.

For soda developers, Dr. Eder mixes 10 parts of pure crystallized soda with 100 parts of water. To use it, he combines 100 c.c. of this solution with 6 c.c. of a pyrogallol solution diluted 1:10, without adding any bromide.

Dr. Stolze’s potassa developer is easier to work with. It comes in two parts. Part 1 contains 200 c.c. of water, 90 g of pure potassium carbonate, and 25 g of sodium sulphite. Part 2 contains 100 c.c. of water, 1½ g of citric acid, 25 g of sodium sulphite, and 12 g of pyrogallol. The second solution lasts longer, so it is preferred. Both solutions keep well in tightly sealed bottles for a while.

