Sometimes his parents took him to parties. No babysitter was needed. Albert would sit on the couch, do math quietly, while other people danced. He liked doing math problems so much that his pencil and paper were his toys.

His sister, Maja, said he could focus even when it was noisy. She remembered him lying on the sofa, picking up a pen, balancing an inkwell on the back of the chair, and solving problems so hard that the noise helped him instead of distracting him.

Einstein was smart, but he was not much more clever than his classmates. He said, “I don’t have special gifts. I am just very curious.”

