Model: Rewritten Excerpt:
The term “mirror stage” means the time when babies and toddlers first see themselves in a mirror. Studies show that babies love looking at their own picture in a mirror, but they don’t realize it’s really them until about 15 to 18 months old. Psychologists have talked a lot about how this is important for how a child learns to think about themselves.

In 1936, a psychologist named Jacques Lacan said that the mirror stage happens when a child is 6 to 18 months old. A few years later he changed his idea. He said the mirror stage was not just a single moment, but a lasting way that people see themselves. Lacan thought that learning to recognize and think about one’s own reflection was a key part of building a person’s identity.

In 1953, Lacan wrote, “The mirror stage is a big deal because it shows a turning point in a child’s thinking.”

