 In the hallway, a door knocked. A quiet voice spoke. Then the knock was heard again on Prescott’s door.

“Come in!” Dick shouted.
An orderly walked in, saluted.

“Orders from the adjutant, sir,” the soldier said, handing Prescott a folded paper. He gave another one to Greg, then saluted and left, knocking on the next door.

“Company drill from one to two‑thirty,” Prescott summarized, looking at the words on the paper. “Practice march from two‑forty‑five to three‑forty‑five. Squad drill from four to the retreat. That’s fast, Greg.”

“Doesn’t it?” Holmes asked, showing little excitement. “The drill and the hike want us, but I have a lot of paperwork to finish before dinner.”

Paperwork is the bane of the company commander. It means keeping, making and signing records, checking accounts, and writing many reports about supplies.”

